{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HZ8qhK25K31qBERPkMCRb3","type":"tool_result","content":"Makefile\nREADME.md\n__pycache__\ncheck_axioms.py\ncheck_lean.py\ncheck_refs.py\ndata\nmain.aux\nmain.bbl\nmain.blg\nmain.fdb_latexmk\nmain.fls\nmain.log\nmain.out\nmain.pdf\nmain.tex\nmake_tables.py\nrefs.bib\nsec1_intro.tex\nsec2_equivalences.tex\nsec3_names.tex\nsec4_search.tex\nsec5_results.tex\nsec6_conclusion.tex\nsec7_data.tex\nsec9a_appendix_lean.tex\nsec9b_appendix_machinery.tex\nsec9c_appendix_names.tex\ntables\n     1\t% Section 5, moved as is from old Sections 4.6-4.7 (loop0009 item 01). Sources:\n     2\t% paper2/solver_fix.md (cost, re-certification), paper2/certificates.md (proof\n     3\t% object), paper2/dataset.md (the dataset). Tables from tables/ (make_tables.py).\n     4\t\\section{Computational results and a dataset}\n     5\t\\label{sec:results}\n     6\t\n     7\t\\subsection{The revised algorithm in practice}\n     8\t\\label{sec:practice}\n     9\t\n    10\tThe revised search changes two tests. The definite move fires on the first\n    11\tplayable $q$ that passes the published test and then the matching of\n    12\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching}; a $q$ that fails the matching is passed over. The\n    13\tbetter move cites $q$ for $r$ only if the same matching succeeds at\n    14\t$\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$. The matching joins at most $\\close-1$ customers to $\\open$\n    15\tstacks and stops at $\\open-1$ edges. Since 1 October 2026 these are the default\n    16\tin both of our solvers, the MOSP customer search and the graph pathwidth solver,\n    17\tin C and in Python; the published rules remain behind a flag.\n    18\t\n    19\t\\begin{table}[t]\n    20\t\\centering\\small\n    21\t\\caption{The cost of the repair: nodes under the published and the repaired\n    22\trules, in paired runs of the same decision in one worker. Totals over the pairs\n    23\tthat finished under both settings; a censored pair says nothing about cost.\n    24\tMOSP rows refute the optimum minus one; the graph row is whole descents at\n    25\t120\\,s each.}\n    26\t\\label{tab:cost}\n    27\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/cost}}\n    28\t\\end{table}\n    29\t\n    30\tTable~\\ref{tab:cost} gives the cost in nodes. The repair changes tree sizes by\n    31\tat most a few percent and never changed an answer. Per node it costs about 2\\% in\n    32\tthe MOSP C engine, measured on six hard instances at 75--125 customers. The\n    33\tpublished test passes and the matching then fails on 0.07--0.27\\% of\n    34\tdefinite-move candidates, a rate that rises with size.\n    35\t\n    36\t\\paragraph{Re-certification.} Of the 6,374 certified optima in our MOSP corpus,\n    37\t6,259 do not depend on the customer search: they rest on an exact subset-lattice\n    38\tcomputation, a checked DRAT refutation, an earlier SAT search, or a lower bound\n    39\tequal to the value. The other 115 rested on the customer search under the\n    40\tpublished rules. The repaired solver has re-refuted the value minus one on all\n    41\t115 of them (as of 6 October 2026), and no value changed. Table~\\ref{tab:split}\n    42\tgives the seven hardest, 125-customer instances at densities 2 and 4, which\n    43\tneeded a split of the root over many cores. The table was read on 3 October\n    44\t2026, when three were still running; all three have since been refuted. The split is proved\n    45\tsound~\\leanref{split}.\n    46\t\n    47\t\\begin{table}[t]\n    48\t\\centering\\small\n    49\t\\caption{Re-certification of the hardest corpus values under the repaired rules,\n    50\tby a split of the root into independent tasks. \\emph{$k$}: the value minus one,\n    51\twhich must be refuted. For an instance in flight, the nodes and core-hours are\n    52\tthose of the finished tasks so far.}\n    53\t\\label{tab:split}\n    54\t\\input{tables/split}\n    55\t\\end{table}\n    56\t\n    57\t\\paragraph{Certificates.} A refutation by the search is a claim about a tree of\n    58\t$10^{11}$ nodes at 125 customers, and nothing in Theorem~\\ref{thm:main} lets a\n    59\treader check that the code ran the search it models. So the search emits a\n    60\tcertificate: the tree, every pruning step with its rule and witness, and for\n    61\teach definite and better step the matching of Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}. An\n    62\tindependent checker, which shares no code with the search, recomputes every\n    63\tpremise from the matrix, verifies each matching (distinct customers, distinct\n    64\tstacks, each stack newly opened by its customer, at least $\\open-1$ edges),\n    65\trequires every chain of covers to end at a child or an old move with no cycle,\n    66\tand replays the tree. Each accepted link is a Lean theorem; that an acyclic\n    67\tcovering by such links is sound is a short paper argument, not yet formal.\n    68\tTable~\\ref{tab:certs} gives the results on the corpus at 9--75 customers.\n    69\tEvery refutation emitted within 60\\,s verifies, 6,275 of 6,286 under the\n    70\t\\texttt{csearch} configuration, and none is rejected; the 35.5\\,MB of\n    71\tcertificates check in 147\\,s. On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}\n    72\trooted at $\\{2\\}$, the published rules emit an exhaustive tree that claims no\n    73\tsolution; a checker of the published premises accepts it, and ours rejects it,\n    74\teven with a largest matching attached (one edge where two are needed).\n    75\t\n    76\t\\begin{table}[t]\n    77\t\\centering\\small\n    78\t\\caption{Certificates for the repaired rules on the certified corpus at 9--75\n    79\tcustomers, refuting the optimum minus one, 60\\,s emission budget.\n    80\t\\emph{Unknown}: emission did not finish. Below 41 customers the\n    81\t\\texttt{default} rows match \\texttt{csearch} and are omitted.}\n    82\t\\label{tab:certs}\n    83\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/certificates}}\n    84\t\\end{table}\n    85\t\n    86\t\\subsection{A dataset with certified pathwidth}\n    87\t\\label{sec:dataset}\n    88\t\n    89\tSection~\\ref{sec:equiv} makes one certified pathwidth an exact answer to every\n    90\tproblem in Table~1's exact core: pathwidth, vertex separation and the vertex\n    91\tseparator game take the width $w$; MOSP, gate matrix layout with multiple\n    92\tfolding, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, narrowness and node search\n    93\ttake $w+1$. We therefore built one dataset from every benchmark collection of\n    94\tthose problems that we could obtain, and the MOSP corpus. The unit is the\n    95\tisomorphism class of the graph, the MOSP graph for a matrix instance: two\n    96\tinstances with isomorphic graphs have the same value under every problem, so the\n    97\tdataset holds one record per class, with its members and their isomorphism maps.\n    98\tEach record carries the graph, the width, its provenance (\\emph{refutation},\n    99\t\\emph{bound} or \\emph{upper bound}), a witness layout of exactly that width and,\n   100\tfor a bound, a minor certificate that replays.\n   101\t\n   102\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   103\t\\centering\\small\n   104\t\\caption{The dataset by collection. \\emph{Classes}: isomorphism classes within\n   105\tthe collection (nauty certificates; 78 graphs above 5,000 vertices by file\n   106\tcontent only). \\emph{Owned}: classes whose first member is in this collection,\n   107\tso the column sums to the dataset's class count. The provenance columns count\n   108\towned classes with a witness layout.}\n   109\t\\label{tab:dataset}\n   110\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/dataset}}\n   111\t\\end{table}\n   112\t\n   113\tTable~\\ref{tab:dataset} gives the counts. Twenty collections, 21,754 instance\n   114\tfiles, reduce to 17,714 classes: the MOSP collections\n   115\t\\citep{SmithGent2005,FaggioliBentivoglio1998,ChuStuckey2009,CarvalhoSoma2015,Frinhani2018},\n   116\televen VLSI gate matrix circuits \\citep{OliveiraLorena2002}, the VSPLIB sets\n   117\t\\citep{Duarte2012} and the Small graphs \\citep{MartiCamposPinana2008}, the Rome\n   118\tgraphs \\citep{DiBattista1997}, and treewidth collections used as pathwidth\n   119\tbenchmarks \\citep{TreewidthLIB,freetdi,PACE2016,PACE2017}. Of the classes, 16,087\n   120\tcarry a certified pathwidth with a witness layout and 862 a witnessed upper\n   121\tbound. An independent checker, sharing no code with the solvers, re-reads all\n   122\t20,987 member files with its own readers, maps each onto its record by\n   123\tisomorphism, re-measures every witness and replays every bound certificate, and\n   124\treports no failure on the 16,949 records.\n   125\t\n   126\tAgainst published values: all 84 Small graphs agree with the pathwidths of\n   127\t\\citet[Tables~4--5]{Mallach2018}, and ten of the eleven VLSI circuits are\n   128\tcertified at their published best-known track counts \\citep[Table~I]{OliveiraLorena2002};\n   129\tthe eleventh, W4 with 202 nets, is open at 28 against 27. One caution about MOSP\n   130\tbenchmark files emerged: the same public repository ships the Carvalho and Soma\n   131\tinstances as customers by patterns and the Chu and Stuckey instances beside them\n   132\tas patterns by customers, and only published densities and values tell them\n   133\tapart. Read the wrong way round, an instance certifies at a different value.\n   134\t\n   135\tWhat is left is priced. One more budget step for every open class that fits our\n   136\tengine (1,024 vertices per component) costs at most 500 core-hours, and on the\n   137\tRome graphs each such step has closed about 40\\% of what it attempted. The\n   138\tlargest MOSP instances, 400 to 1,000 customers, and the 154 classes above 1,024\n   139\tvertices are beyond an exact search. Refutation-certified records rest on the\n   140\trepaired search; for those within reach of the certificate emitter, the proof\n   141\tobject above is the route by which a third party can check them without our code.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ac98659f-e13b-43d0-af66-0677acbe2e7e","uuid":"d9e91085-0ba8-47b9-a762-de727dcd37b0","timestamp":"2026-10-06T17:26:14.144Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Makefile\nREADME.md\n__pycache__\ncheck_axioms.py\ncheck_lean.py\ncheck_refs.py\ndata\nmain.aux\nmain.bbl\nmain.blg\nmain.fdb_latexmk\nmain.fls\nmain.log\nmain.out\nmain.pdf\nmain.tex\nmake_tables.py\nrefs.bib\nsec1_intro.tex\nsec2_equivalences.tex\nsec3_names.tex\nsec4_search.tex\nsec5_results.tex\nsec6_conclusion.tex\nsec7_data.tex\nsec9a_appendix_lean.tex\nsec9b_appendix_machinery.tex\nsec9c_appendix_names.tex\ntables\n     1\t% Section 5, moved as is from old Sections 4.6-4.7 (loop0009 item 01). Sources:\n     2\t% paper2/solver_fix.md (cost, re-certification), paper2/certificates.md (proof\n     3\t% object), paper2/dataset.md (the dataset). Tables from tables/ (make_tables.py).\n     4\t\\section{Computational results and a dataset}\n     5\t\\label{sec:results}\n     6\t\n     7\t\\subsection{The revised algorithm in practice}\n     8\t\\label{sec:practice}\n     9\t\n    10\tThe revised search changes two tests. The definite move fires on the first\n    11\tplayable $q$ that passes the published test and then the matching of\n    12\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching}; a $q$ that fails the matching is passed over. The\n    13\tbetter move cites $q$ for $r$ only if the same matching succeeds at\n    14\t$\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$. The matching joins at most $\\close-1$ customers to $\\open$\n    15\tstacks and stops at $\\open-1$ edges. Since 1 October 2026 these are the default\n    16\tin both of our solvers, the MOSP customer search and the graph pathwidth solver,\n    17\tin C and in Python; the published rules remain behind a flag.\n    18\t\n    19\t\\begin{table}[t]\n    20\t\\centering\\small\n    21\t\\caption{The cost of the repair: nodes under the published and the repaired\n    22\trules, in pa