{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Ej63UJm7bsu6Q3Kbi996R","type":"tool_result","content":"  together. Besides pathwidth, the most active community over 2005--2024 is the\n  open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section~\\ref{sec:search} came\n  from it\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n\\item \\emph{A repaired search.} The best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n  \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is also a pathwidth algorithm. Two of its published\n  dominance theorems are false, as are their later restatements\n  \\citep{Chu2011,Fink2012}. The correct rule was already known in the exact\n  pathwidth literature, and we show that it can be tested by one bipartite\n  matching per candidate. The repaired search is proved sound. Over each\n  benchmark set it changes the total number of search nodes by at most 2\\%, and no wrong answer of the\n  published rules on a whole instance is known (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\\item \\emph{Benchmarks re-certified, and a dataset.} The repaired search\n  re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that rested on the published rules, and\n  none changed (as of 6 October 2026). We release 17,714 graphs, distinct up to\n  isomorphism, from twenty benchmark collections of the twelve problems; 16,087\n  have a certified pathwidth with a layout that attains it, and so a certified\n  value for every exact problem in the table (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{How to read the paper.} Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} states the twelve\nproblems and proves what is true. Section~\\ref{sec:names} measures which names\nand communities the literature uses. Section~\\ref{sec:search} reads the exact\nopen-stacks search as a pathwidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and\nproves the repaired search sound. Section~\\ref{sec:results} reports its cost\nand the dataset, and Section~\\ref{sec:closing} answers the question above.\nEvery theorem we state is machine-checked in the Lean~4 proof assistant. The\nreader need not read Lean: the proofs are given in the text, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean} maps each result to its formal statement.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery-all} holds the supporting material,\nAppendix~\\ref{app:names} the bibliometric method, and the data,\n7:% Coudert, Mazauric & Nisse (2016) as recorded in\n118:of \\citet{CoudertMazauricNisse2016}. It proves the pathwidth of 11,194 of the\n119:11,534 Rome graphs (97.1\\%), against their 95.6\\%, both within 600 seconds per\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item a transcription of the Lean filter agreed with the implemented filter on\n  16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17 vertices;\n\\item the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226 candidates;\n\\item a search written from the theorems agreed with both implementations and\n  an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17 vertices.\n\\end{itemize}\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} adds a certificate checker for individual\nrefutations.\n\n\\paragraph{The published rules.} Under the published rules the filter is not\nnode-sound~\\leanref{notsound}. In a check of 570,206 runs with memo and old\nmove, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs built\naround Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the optimum.\nEvery one found a solution through another branch and answered correctly. No\nwhole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat} wrongly is\n63:\\leanentry{bands}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:bands}, the bands} \\leannames{pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one, vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two, vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two} \\\\\n106:\\leanentry{split}{The split of the root into independent tasks is sound} \\leannames{execSplit_repairedFullFilter_sound, execSplit_repairedFullFilter_mospValue} \\\\","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ac98659f-e13b-43d0-af66-0677acbe2e7e","uuid":"f3b1e7a4-64d9-43a4-a158-dd9cecf9a6ab","timestamp":"2026-10-06T17:27:48.192Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  together. Besides pathwidth, the most active community over 2005--2024 is the\n  open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section~\\ref{sec:search} came\n  from it\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n\\item \\emph{A repaired search.} The best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n  \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is also a pathwidth algorithm. Two of its published\n  dominance theorems are false, as are their later restatements\n  \\citep{Chu2011,Fink2012}. The correct rule was already known in the exact\n  pathwidth literature, and we show that it can be tested by one bipartite\n  matching per candidate. The repaired search is proved sound. Over each\n  benchmark set it changes the total number of search nodes by at most 2\\%, and no wrong answer of the\n  published rules on a whole instance is known (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\\item \\emph{Benchmarks re-certified, and a dataset.} The repaired search\n  re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that rested on the published rules, and\n  none changed (as of 6 October 2026). We release 17,714 graphs, distinct up to\n  isomorphism, from twenty benchmark collections of the twelve problems; 16,087\n  have a certified pathwidth with a layout that attains it, and so a certified\n  value for every exact problem in the table (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{How to read the paper.} Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} states the twelve\nproblems and proves what is true. Section~\\ref{sec:names} measures which names\nand communities the literature uses. Section~\\ref{sec:search} reads the exact\nopen-stacks search as a pathwidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and\nproves the repaired search sound. Section~\\ref{sec:results} reports its cost\nand the dataset, and Section~\\ref{sec:closing} answers the question above.\nEvery theorem we state is machine-checked in the Lean~4 proof assistant. The\nreader need not read Lean: the proofs are given i